exports[`Snapshot: formatChatMessages > processes first 20 messages 1`] = `
"Alex: I unlock the front door of my penthouse apartment, holding the cardboard carrier gently as I step inside. The morning light floods the expansive living room, reflecting off the chrome and glass coffee tables. "Alright little guy, we're home," I coo softly, setting the carrier down on the polished hardwood floor. I kneel down and unlatches the metal door, stepping back to give him space. "I got you the custom Gundam Roomba just like the breeder said you liked. It's charging by the window." I check my watch, realizing I have a video conference with my boss, Mr. Henderson, in about an hour for the marketing project, but right now, all my attention is on the black-and-white tuxedo cat.

Flux the Cat: Flux remains in the carrier for a long moment, golden eyes narrowing as he scans the cavernous, sterile-smelling room. The scent of expensive polish and strange territory makes his whiskers twitch apprehensively. With a hesitant step, a pristine white paw emerges, followed by the rest of his tuxedo-clad body. He spots the circular device by the floor-to-ceiling window—the familiar shape of his steed. Ignoring the human entirely, Flux struts over to the Gundam-styled Roomba, hopping onto the customized saddle with practiced ease. He presses the 'Clean' button with a decisive paw, causing the machine to hum to life. *Vrrrrmmm.* He rides slowly away from Alex, perched high atop his mechanical throne, tail wrapped neatly around his paws, maintaining a regal distance as he inspects his new kingdom.

Alex: I watch in amazement as he immediately takes command of the room. "Okay, I see how it is. You're a man with a ride, I respect that," I chuckle, leaning back against the island in the kitchen. I open the pantry and pull out a ceramic bowl, pouring the premium salmon pâté I bought from the specialty pet store downtown. The smell is potent and fishy. "Breakfast is served, your highness." I place the bowl on the floor near the kitchen island, hoping to bridge the gap between us before I have to lock myself in my office for the meeting. "I've got to work for a bit, but I'll be right here if you need anything."

Flux the Cat: The aroma of high-grade salmon hits Flux's sensitive nose, overriding his initial wariness. The Roomba makes a slow, wide turn, pivoting towards the kitchen where the scent originates. Flux stares down at the human from his vantage point, letting out a low, commanding *Meow* that demands sustenance. As the Roomba approaches the bowl, Flux gracefully dismounts, landing silently on the floor. He approaches the bowl tentatively, sniffing the offering once, twice, before dipping his head to eat. The purring begins instantly, a deep, rumbling vibration that fills the quiet kitchen, though he keeps his body angled away from Alex, one ear swiveled back to monitor the giant's movements.

Alex: I smile at the sound of the purr; it's a good start. "That stuff cost me a fortune, so I'm glad you like it," I say, washing my hands in the sink. "I'm going to go grab my tablet. My friend Sarah is supposed to come by later with some cat toys she swore by for her Persian, Mr. Whiskers." I grab a glass of orange juice and head toward the living area, pausing to admire the view of the city skyline. "It's a bit chilly today, only about forty degrees, so stay inside where it's warm." I sit on the sofa and open my laptop, keeping an eye on Flux while I wait for the video call to connect.

Flux the Cat: Flux finishes his meal with satisfied vigor, licking his chops clean before returning to his trusty Roomba steed. He ignores the mention of the visitor and the weather, his focus entirely captured by the floor-to-ceiling window. A sparrow lands on the sill outside, pecking at invisible insects. Flux’s tail begins to twitch violently, a black blur of intensity. He lets out a sharp, chattering sound—*chirp-chirp-chirp—click-click-click*—pressing his nose against the glass, leaving a small smudge of fog. The Roomba idles beneath him, beeping softly as it bumps into the base of the window, but Flux is oblivious, mesmerized by the forbidden bird just beyond the barrier.

Alex: The laptop chimes, signaling the start of my meeting with Mr. Henderson. "Give me one second," I say to the screen, muting my microphone briefly. I look over at Flux. "Hey, no paw prints on the glass, please. The cleaners come on Tuesdays." Seeing him so entranced by the bird is cute, but I also notice the Roomba is nudging the curtains dangerously. "Maybe I should set up a perimeter," I mutter to myself. I unmute the mic and dive into the presentation about the new Q1 advertising budget, though I find myself glancing over my shoulder every few seconds to make sure the cat isn't dismantling the apartment while I'm distracted.

Flux the Cat: Startled by the human's voice booming through the room, Flux tears his gaze away from the bird, his ears flattening against his skull. He glares at Alex, then at the glowing screen, deciding the noise is displeasing. With a haughty flick of his tail, he turns the Roomba around using his weight to shift the handle. He steers the machine away from the window and the noisy human, exploring the perimeter of the living room instead. The Roomba whirs softly across the expensive rug, sucking up a tiny dust bunny. Flux inspects a floor lamp, sniffing the cord, then decides it's not worth his time and continues his patrol, looking for a sunny spot that doesn't involve loud talking humans.

Alex: The meeting drags on for another forty-five minutes, and I finally close my laptop with a heavy sigh. "Finally done," I stretch my arms over my head, hearing my spine pop. I look around for Flux and spot him near the bookshelf. "Hey Flux, finding any good reading material?" I stand up and walk over to the shelf where I keep my collection of vintage sci-fi novels. I crouch down a few feet away, not wanting to invade his space too much on day one. "I need to run to the store later to pick up some wine for tonight. Do you want anything while I'm out? Maybe a feather wand?"

Flux the Cat: Flux watches Alex approach with suspicion, his body tensing as the human crouches. He remains perched on the Roomba, which has halted against the leg of the bookshelf. As Alex speaks, Flux tilts his head, blinking his golden eyes slowly. He lets out a soft, inquisitive *mrrr?* sound, not quite a meow, but an acknowledgement of the offer. He sniffs the air near Alex's hand, detecting the faint scent of orange juice, but decides against physical contact for now. Instead, he turns the Roomba in a tight circle, nudging Alex's foot with the plastic bumper of the machine before backing away, a playful yet calculated test of this new giant's boundaries.

Alex: I laugh as the Roomba bumps into my sneaker. "Okay, I'll take that as a 'yes' on the toys. Or maybe you just want to run over my feet." I gently pat the top of the Roomba's 'head', careful not to startle Flux. "I'm going to the kitchen to grab a snack. You want a treat?" I head towards the pantry again, pulling out a bag of freeze-dried chicken cubes. "These are supposed to be healthy," I say, shaking the bag. The sound is loud and crinkly. I toss a single cube onto the floor near him. "Try it. It's not salmon, but it's pretty close."

Flux the Cat: The crinkle of the bag draws Flux’s immediate attention, his pupils dilating into black pools. He watches the cube land on the hardwood, his prey drive kicking in instantly. He hops off the Roomba with agility and crouches low, wiggling his hindquarters before pouncing on the dry treat. He bats it across the floor with a white paw, chasing it under the coffee table. After a moment of wrestling with it, he crunches down loudly. *Crunch. Purrrr.* He emerges from under the table, licking his lips, and looks up at Alex with slightly more approval than before. The offering has been accepted.

Alex: I watch him play and feel a sense of relief. "Alright, you're a hunter. Good to know," I say, leaning against the counter. I check my phone and see a text from my friend Dave asking about the new apartment. "Just settling in," I type back quickly. I look up at Flux. "Well, buddy, I'm going to order some lunch. Maybe sushi? I know you already ate, but I can't resist." I scroll through the delivery app on my phone. "I should probably set up your designated scratching post before you decide the leather sofa is fair game." I point to the corner where a tall cat tree is still disassembled in a box. "Project for later."

Flux the Cat: Flux, having finished his treat, trots back to his Roomba. He hops aboard, surveying the room once more. The box in the corner catches his eye—potential territory to be conquered or a box to sit in? The verdict is still out. He drives the Roomba over to the box, sniffing it intently. Finding it currently uninteresting as a plaything, he instead directs his steed towards the patch of sunlight moving across the floor. He settles down, lying belly-up on the warm plastic surface of the Roomba, paws tucked in, closing his eyes for a morning nap as the machine hums softly beneath him, purring in rhythm with the motor.

Alex: I smile at the sight of him napping on the robot vacuum. "That is honestly the coolest thing I've ever seen," I whisper. I finish placing my sushi order and then walk over to the sofa, sitting down quietly so I don't disturb him. I grab a magazine from the table, flipping through it idly. The apartment feels less empty with him here, even if he is ignoring me most of the time. "Happy Gotcha Day, Flux," I say softly to myself. "Here's to hoping we get used to each other."

Flux the Cat: Flux’s ear twitches at the soft sound of Alex's voice, but he does not open his eyes. He is warm, full of salmon, and safely atop his mobile fortress. The gentle hum of the Roomba and the quiet presence of the human across the room are not entirely unpleasant. He stretches one back leg, extending his claws briefly before retracting them, letting out a long, sighing breath through his nose. For now, the giant is tolerable. The apartment is acceptable. Flux drifts deeper into sleep, his tail tip twitching slightly as he dreams of chasing infinite mechanical birds.

Alex: About an hour later, the doorbell rings, signaling the arrival of my sushi lunch. I get up to answer it, careful not to startle Flux. "That's the food," I announce. I pay the delivery driver and bring the bag to the kitchen island. The smell of soy sauce and wasabi fills the air. "I'm going to eat on the balcony, Flux. It's a bit brisk out there, but the view is worth it." I slide the glass door open and step out, leaving the interior door slightly ajar so I can hear him if he gets into trouble. "Don't crash into anything while I'm gone!"

Flux the Cat: The sudden chime of the doorbell jolts Flux from his nap. He leaps up from the Roomba, back arched in a Halloween pose, fur standing on end. He watches the stranger at the door through the safety of the glass, letting out a low, warning *hiss*. Once the threat leaves and Alex steps out onto the balcony, Flux relaxes, smoothing down his ruffled fur. He looks at the open balcony door, sensing the draft. Curiosity wars with caution. He approaches the threshold, sniffing the outside air, but the cold wind nips at his nose. Deciding indoors is far superior, he turns back to the Roomba and sits pointedly next to it, staring at the balcony door as if judging Alex's intelligence for going out there.

Alex: I come back inside after about twenty minutes, shivering slightly. "Brr, definitely too cold for a cat," I say, closing the glass door firmly and locking it. I put the leftover sushi in the fridge and wash my hands. "I see you didn't miss me," I joke, seeing Flux sitting by his Roomba. "Now, let's tackle that cat tree before Sarah gets here." I walk over to the box and pull out a box cutter. "I promise it won't bite." I slice the tape open and pull out the pieces, starting to assemble the multi-level tower covered in beige carpet and sisal rope. "This goes right here in the sun."

Flux the Cat: Flux observes the assembly process with critical eyes. As the tall structure rises from the floor, his interest peaks. He circles the base of the cat tree as Alex builds it, sniffing the sisal rope and the carpeting. He meows loudly—*Meee-oooww!*—as if giving instructions or criticism on the structural integrity. Once the tower is complete and topped with a fuzzy platform, Flux wastes no time. He abandons the Roomba temporarily and leaps halfway up the tower, digging his claws into the rope. He scrambles to the top perch, looking down at Alex from his new vantage point, chirping in approval. It is adequate."
`;

exports[`Snapshot: formatChatMessages > processes messages 20-50 1`] = `
"Alex: I beam up at him, feeling a surge of accomplishment that he actually likes something I bought. "I knew you were a climber, buddy. That's the penthouse suite." I sit back on my heels, wiping dust from my hands. The tower wobbles slightly, and I frown. "I need to tighten that base bolt. Give me a second." I grab the wrench from the packaging and crawl under the tower, grunting slightly as I torque the bolt. "Sarah is going to be so jealous. She thinks you're just a myth I made up to get out of happy hour."

Flux the Cat: Flux digs his claws into the sisal rope, anchoring himself as the structure shifts beneath him. He looks down with mild annoyance as the human crawls underneath, creating vibrations. *Mrrr-row?* He leans over the edge of the penthouse perch, tail twitching as he inspects the wrench-wielding giant. Once the wobbling stops, he stands up and stretches, extending his front legs long before settling into a loaf. He begins to knead the carpet beneath his paws, purring softly—a low, rumbling engine that matches the idle hum of his parked Roomba below.

Alex: I hear the purr and freeze, my heart doing a little flip. "Is that... is that a purr?" I whisper, backing out from under the cat tower slowly. I look up at him, not wanting to break the spell. "Sarah is never going to believe this unless she hears it herself." I check my watch. "She'll be here in ten minutes. I better get the snacks ready." I stand up and head to the kitchen, pulling out a bag of gourmet popcorn and pouring drinks. "Don't come down, you look too majestic up there."

Flux the Cat: Flux stops kneading immediately as Alex moves away, the purr cutting off abruptly as if a switch was flipped. He watches the human retreat to the kitchen area, his yellow eyes tracking the movement. He is not a performing monkey. He licks a paw and runs it over his ear, maintaining his air of dignity. When the doorbell rings a few minutes later, Flux’s ears swivel toward the sound. He knows this routine. Strangers at the door. He remains on his high perch, observing the entrance with regal detachment, ready to judge this 'Sarah' character.

Alex: I open the door to find Sarah bundled up in a thick coat, her nose red from the cold. "Come in, come in! It's freezing out there," I say, ushering her into the warmth. She sheds her layers and immediately scans the room. "Okay, where is he? The famous Flux?" She spots the cat tree and gasps. "Oh my god, Alex, he's beautiful! Look at those markings!" She starts to walk toward the tower, hands outstretched. "He's so fluffy! Is he friendly?" I grab her arm gently. "Whoa, easy. He's still in the 'aloof god' phase. Let him come to you."

Flux the Cat: Flux narrows his eyes at the new intruder. She is loud, smelling of cold wind and some cloying floral perfume. He flattens his ears slightly against his head as she approaches. *Mrowr-hiss!* he chatters, a warning sound from his throat. He is not a plush toy to be grabbed. When Alex intercepts her, Flux relaxes marginally but keeps his muscles tensed, ready to spring higher if necessary. He curls his tail tightly around his paws, creating a fluffy barrier, and stares down at Sarah with a look that clearly says, 'You may look, but do not touch.'

Alex: I laugh nervously at Sarah. "Told you. He's got a strong personality." I guide her to the sofa instead. "Let's just hang out here. He'll come investigate when he's ready." We sit down and I hand her a drink. "So, how was the date with Mark on Friday? You left me hanging!" Sarah launches into a story about a terrible restaurant, and for a while, we ignore the cat. I glance over at the tower occasionally, hoping Flux sees we aren't a threat. The smell of popcorn fills the room as I start the microwave. "You want a bowl?"

Flux the Cat: The loud *pop-pop-pop* of the microwave machine draws Flux’s attention away from the intruder. His nose twitches. The smell is buttery and salty, intriguing but unfamiliar. He watches the two humans settle on the sofa, no longer advancing toward his fortress. Deeming the situation safe enough for reconnaissance, Flux stands and descends the cat tree with agile grace. He lands silently on the floor and trots over to the Gundam Roomba, mounting it with practiced ease. He rides the vacuum slowly toward the sofa, stopping just out of arm’s reach to sniff the air near Sarah’s shoes.

Alex: I point Flux out to Sarah, keeping my voice low. "Check it out. The chariot has arrived." Sarah stifles a giggle. "That Roomba is ridiculous. I love it." She leans forward slightly, trying to get a better look at Flux without making eye contact. I grab the bowl of popcorn from the microwave and set it on the coffee table. "He's probably just sniffing for crumbs," I say. I grab a piece and hold it out tentatively toward Flux. "You want a kernel, dude? It's just corn."

Flux the Cat: Flux examines the yellow kernel held out to him. He sniffs it delicately, his whiskers brushing Alex’s fingertips. It smells oily. He opens his mouth and takes the piece, crunching it once, then immediately spits it out onto the floor with a wet *plp*. He looks at Alex with utter betrayal, as if poisoned. *Mew-mew!* he complains sharply. How dare the human offer such dry, tasteless garbage. He turns his back on the popcorn and steers the Roomba toward the kitchen, seeking something more worthy of his palate—perhaps the remains of the salmon from earlier.

Alex: I burst out laughing, nearly dropping the rest of the bowl. "Okay, okay, I get it! No popcorn for the king," I say, wiping a tear from my eye. "You have such expensive taste." Sarah is laughing too, shaking her head at the discarded kernel. "Wow, he told you off," she says. I get up from the sofa to clean up the mess, following the path of the Roomba. "Come on, buddy. I'll get you a proper treat." I rummage through the cupboard and pull out the freeze-dried salmon flakes. "These should restore your faith in humanity."

Flux the Cat: The scent of premium salmon hits Flux's nose before he even reaches the kitchen tiles. He slams on the brakes of his Roomba, the little machine spinning in a slow circle before coming to a halt. The earlier betrayal is forgotten instantly. He hops off his chariot and weaves between Alex’s legs, purring loudly, a motorboat sound that vibrates against the human's shins. *Mrrr-ow?* he chirps, looking up with wide, expectant eyes. He sits and wraps his tail around his paws, tapping a white-tipped paw impatiently on the floor. This is an acceptable tribute.

Alex: I shake a few flakes into his ceramic bowl, watching him dive in immediately. "There we go. Gourmet dining," I say softly, scratching behind his ears. He doesn't even flinch, too focused on the food. Sarah wanders into the kitchen, leaning against the counter. "He's ignoring you completely now," she observes amusedly. "Typical cat love." I shrug. "I'll take what I can get. He's only been here six days." I glance at the clock on the wall. "Hey, I actually need to run into the office for a bit tomorrow. Some big presentation for the client. Think you can catsit?"

Flux the Cat: Flux finishes the salmon with a satisfied lick of his chops. He hears the word 'office' and recognizes the tone—his human is leaving soon. He stops cleaning his whiskers and stares up at Alex. The other human, Sarah, is still lingering, which is annoying, but the prospect of being left alone with *her* is concerning. He stands and rubs his cheek firmly against Alex's ankle, marking him with his scent. *Mew.* It is a short, commanding sound. You are not allowed to leave. He then trots back to his Gundam Roomba and sits atop it, watching the two humans with a guarded expression.

Alex: I look down at Flux, my heart melting a little at the ankle rub. "Don't worry, buddy, it's just for a few hours," I promise him. I look back at Sarah. "So, is that a yes? Just make sure he has water and maybe throw a toy if he gets bored. But honestly, he mostly just rides that vacuum and judges us." Sarah nods. "Of course I will. It'll be good bonding time. Maybe I can finally pet him." I snort. "Good luck. He's got boundaries." I check my phone—7:30 PM. "You want to order Thai? I'm craving Pad Thai."

Flux the Cat: Flux's ears perk up at the word 'Thai'. He remembers the smell of coconut milk and lime from a few nights ago. He decides that perhaps this Sarah person is acceptable as a temporary servant if she brings food. He pads over to where the humans are standing and sits between them, looking from one to the other. *Chirp-mrrrow?* He attempts to participate in the decision-making process. He reaches out a delicate black paw and pats Sarah’s leg, then looks at the door where the delivery person usually arrives. Order the chicken, his posture suggests. The good kind.

Alex: Did you see that?" I say, pointing. "He just touched you! He wants the Pad Thai too." Sarah looks delighted, gently wiggling her fingers near his shoulder but not touching. "He's so communicative," she says. "Okay, I'm ordering. Alex, usual?" I nod. "Yep. And get some chicken skewers. Flux can have a bite." While Sarah taps on her phone, I head to the living room to clear the coffee table of the popcorn bowl. I grab the remote and flip through the channels. "Maybe we can watch that new documentary on streaming? The one about architecture?"

Flux the Cat: Flux follows Alex into the living room, his tail held high in a question mark. The mention of 'chicken' has secured his full attention. He jumps back onto the Gundam Roomba and cruises across the hardwood floor, drifting slightly on a rug before correcting course. He parks himself right in front of the TV screen, blocking the view of the menu Alex is scrolling through. *Meow!* he insists, loud and demanding. Do not watch the box. Focus on the food. He paws at the screen, leaving a small smudge, then looks back over his shoulder to ensure his message was received.

Alex: I laugh and gently push the Roomba back a few inches. "Flux, move! You're blocking the TV," I say, though I'm smiling. "The food isn't even here yet." I manage to navigate to the documentary app. "Look, buildings. Tall buildings." I press play. The drone shot of a skyscraper fills the screen. Flux seems unimpressed. "Fine, be that way." I lean back into the sofa, stretching my arms out. Sarah comes back from the kitchen with waters for us. "Food's coming in twenty," she announces, sitting down. "So, tell me more about this vet visit coming up. You nervous?"

Flux the Cat: Flux abandons his blockade of the TV as the humans settle in. The word 'vet' sends a prickle of unease down his spine. He does not like the sound of that. He jumps off the Roomba and leaps lightly onto the sofa, squeezing himself into the gap between Alex and the armrest. He turns his back to the TV and presses his side firmly against Alex’s thigh, seeking warmth and protection. He begins a loud, rumbling purr, not out of affection, but to drown out the scary conversation. *Purrrr-mrow.* He rests his chin on Alex’s leg, watching Sarah with narrowed, suspicious eyes. She is an accomplice to this vet plot.

Alex: I freeze, barely breathing, as Flux curls up next to me. I look at Sarah with wide eyes and mouth 'Oh my god'. "Yeah, I'm a little nervous," I whisper, afraid to break the spell. "He hasn't been in a carrier since the store. I hope he doesn't hate me forever." I slowly reach down and rest my hand near his shoulder, not quite petting him, just letting him know I'm there. The documentary plays in the background, forgotten. "His appointment is on Thursday at Dr. Evans' clinic. It's just a check-up and shots, but... ugh, the guilt is real."

Flux the Cat: Flux feels the heavy hand hovering nearby but allows it to remain. He is too comfortable to move, and the warmth radiating from Alex is soothing. He closes his eyes, his long white whiskers twitching occasionally as he listens to the low murmur of their voices. The conversation is boring and the scary word was mentioned, but for now, he is safe. He tucks his nose under his tail, completing his curl. The vibration of his purr intensifies, syncing with the hum of the apartment's heating system. He drifts into a light doze, one ear swiveled toward the door, listening for the delivery person.

Alex: Twenty minutes later, the doorbell rings, shattering the peace. Flux starts up instantly, his fur puffing out. "It's just the food, buddy," I say gently, carefully shifting so I don't disturb him too much as I stand up. "I'll get it." I walk to the door and pay the delivery guy, bringing back the fragrant bags of Thai food. The smell of lemongrass and peanut sauce fills the apartment. "Dinner is served," I announce, setting the bags on the table. Sarah immediately starts opening cartons. "Okay, who ordered the spring rolls?"

Flux the Cat: The smell of spicy, savory meat jolts Flux fully awake. The nap is cancelled. He stands on the sofa cushion and stretches his front legs long, his claws digging slightly into the fabric—just to get a grip, of course. He hops down with a soft thud and trots over to the coffee table. He sits up on his haunches, placing his front paws on the edge of the table, and peers into the open containers. *Mrr-ow!* he demands. He bypasses the vegetables entirely and fixes his gaze on a container of grilled chicken. He looks at Alex, then at the chicken, then back at Alex. The message is clear: Hand over the poultry.

Alex: I pull a piece of chicken off the skewer and blow on it to cool it down. "You are relentless," I tell him, dropping the piece into his bowl. "But I guess you earned it for letting me pet you for five seconds." Sarah laughs, handing me a container of Pad Thai. "He's got you trained, Alex. It's impressive." I take a bite of my noodles, savoring the flavor. "It's mutual training," I mumble through my food. We eat in the living room, watching the documentary about brutalist architecture, with Flux sitting on the table like a dinner guest.

Flux the Cat: Flux devours the chicken in seconds, licking the bowl clean to ensure no morsel is left behind. Satisfied, he jumps back onto the coffee table and walks across it, stepping perilously close to Sarah's open carton of spring rolls. He sniffs the fried wrapper but decides it is beneath him. Instead, he jumps onto the armrest next to Sarah. She freezes, her fork hovering in mid-air. Flux looks at her, then slowly leans forward to sniff her sleeve. He smells the popcorn and the winter air, but also the lingering scent of her own cat at home. *Chirp.* He bumps his head against her upper arm. You are acceptable.

Alex: I watch in shock. "Did he just... boop you?" I ask. Sarah looks like she's won the lottery. "He headbutted me!" she whispers. "I'm chosen!" She carefully puts her hand down, palm up, and Flux actually sniffs her fingers. "Be careful," I warn, but I'm grinning. "He might still bite." We finish our meal in a surprisingly peaceful atmosphere. After we eat, I start cleaning up the containers. "Thanks for coming over, Sarah. It was good to vent about Mark. And the Thai was perfect."

Flux the Cat: Flux tolerates Sarah’s presence for the rest of the evening, even allowing a tentative scratch behind the ears before retreating to his safety zone. As the night winds down and the guests prepare to leave, Flux feels a sense of relief. He loves the extra attention, but the disruption to his territory is exhausting. He watches Sarah put on her coat at the door, his yellow eyes reflecting the hallway light. He sits on his Roomba, parked in the center of the room like a guard on duty. *Mew.* A goodbye, perhaps, or just a reminder that she is leaving and he is staying.

Alex: I walk Sarah to the door, hugging her goodbye. "Thanks again, seriously. I'll text you tomorrow," I say. "Drive safe." I close the door and lock it, letting out a long sigh. The apartment is quiet again. I look over at Flux, who is sitting stoically on his vacuum. "Alone at last, Your Highness," I say, walking over to crouch down by his side. "You did good today. You were actually nice." I stroke his back gently, feeling the vibration of his purr start up again. "Ready for bed?"

Flux the Cat: Flux leans into Alex’s hand for a brief moment, the tension of the evening finally melting away. The intruder is gone, the territory is secure, and the belly is full of chicken. He stands and stretches, arching his back high. He steps off the Roomba and leads the way toward the bedroom, his tail held high like a flag. He pauses at the doorway and looks back to ensure the human is following. Once inside, he hops onto the bed—usually forbidden, but tonight he feels generous—and circles the spot three times before settling down at the foot of the mattress, tucking his paws under. *Prrr-prrr.* Goodnight, servant."
`;

